<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Himno de Riego</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">written between 31 January and 6 February <a href="/years/1820">1820</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">In 1820 Colonel Rafael del Riego led an uprising that restored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>'s liberal constitution of 1812 and opened the period known as the Trienio Liberal (1820–1823).</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Himno de Riego, or Riego's Anthem, dates from the Trienio Liberal and is named in honour of Colonel Rafael del Riego, a figure of the uprising that restored the liberal constitution of 1812. Its lyrics were written by Evaristo Fernández de San Miguel, lieutenant colonel of the flying column headed by Riego, and its music is typically attributed to José Melchor Gomis. San Miguel wrote the words between 31 January and 6 February 1820 in the town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeciras">Algeciras</a>, after Riego had rejected another text by Antonio Alcalá Galiano.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The Cortes Generales approved a decree declaring it the national anthem of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> on 7 April 1822. King Fernando VII signed the decree at Aranjuez two days later, and it was published in the Gaceta de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> on 14 April. In October 1823 the king overthrew the liberal government and repealed the constitution, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcha-real">Marcha Real</a> resumed its role as national anthem. During the First Republic of 1873 to 1874 the song held a discreet place, since the most popular anthems of the time were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-marseillaise">La Marseillaise</a> and the Himno de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-garibaldi">Garibaldi</a>.</p><p>By the Second Republic of 1931 to 1939 it had regained popularity and become the main institutional representative of the government, although it was never declared the official anthem of the Second Republic. One opinion holds that the republicans did not consider the declaration of 1822 repealed. A shortened version of verses one, three and seven of the original was used then, and another of the first and last verses. The Second Republican government in exile went on using it until it was dissolved in 1977.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-civil-war">Spanish Civil War</a> a satirical version with rude lyrics was extremely popular among Spaniards, particularly in Catalonia, and it reflected Republican dislike of the church and the monarchy. After the war Franco's detractors and enemies continued to sing it.</p><p>The anthem has also been played by mistake instead of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcha-real">Marcha Real</a>, at a qualifying match for the European championship in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> on 1 October 1967 and at the Davis Cup finals in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> in 2003, when Spanish sports authorities lodged an official protest. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/che-guevara">Che Guevara</a> claimed that when the bell of Cusco Cathedral was rededicated at the expense of the Francoist government, the bell was made to play the Himno de Riego by mistake.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Himno de Riego” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Republic adopted the <b>Himno de Riego</b> as its national anthem and a tricolour of red, yellow and purple as the flag of Spain.</blockquote><a href="/empire/second-spanish-republic">Second Spanish Republic · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1935 the Falangists wanted a stirring song of their own to answer the &quot;<b>Himno de Riego</b>&quot;, the official anthem of the Second Spanish Republic, and &quot;A las Barricadas&quot;, a very popular anarchist song.</blockquote><a href="/songs/cara-al-sol">Cara al Sol</a></li><li><blockquote>Che Guevara claimed that when the bell of Cusco Cathedral in Peru was rededicated in 1952, at the Francoist government's expense, after a 1950 earthquake, it played the <b>Himno de Riego</b> instead of the Spanish anthem, to the dismay of Spanish officials.</blockquote><a href="/songs/himno-de-riego">Himno de Riego</a></li><li><blockquote>The Second Spanish Republic used the <b>Himno de Riego</b> instead.</blockquote><a href="/songs/marcha-real">Marcha Real</a></li></ul></section>
